{"content_hash": "7abd1287bba27042", "version_id": 703, "article_id": 71, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "Japan launches mission to bring back a piece of Mars' moon", "title_zh": "日本发射探测器，要去火星卫星上挖土带回地球", "has_translation": true, "topic": "science", "source": "bbc_science", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmvgxvkv54w1o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1697689806591-e5e4269f10eb?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8OXx8bWlzc2lvbiUyMHNldCUyMGJsYXN0fGVufDB8MHx8fDE3OTIzNjYxMjd8MA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Behnam Mohsenzadeh on Unsplash", "author": "Behnam Mohsenzadeh", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@bmohsenzadeh?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/a-bunch-of-smoke-is-billowing-out-of-a-building-Q5vwV1Xyor4?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(235, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(273, 46%, 48%)", "label": "SCIENCE", "seed": "b923fc532535"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-19", "word_count": 203, "reading_minutes": 1.0, "lead": "A Japanese spacecraft is going to Mars' moon Phobos to collect dust and bring it home. If it works, scientists will finally study a piece of the Red Planet's neighbourhood in their labs.", "lead_zh": "日本的一艘探测器正飞往火星卫星火卫一，准备采集尘土带回地球。如果成功，科学家将首次在实验室里研究来自火星附近的样本。", "preview": "The mission is led by the Japanese Space Agency and launched from Japan on Monday. The spacecraft will reach Phobos in August 2027. If it su", "preview_zh": "这项任务由日本宇宙航空研究开发机构主导，探测器于周一从日本发射。它将在2027年8月抵达火卫一。如果成功，这将是火星或其卫星的样本首次被带回地球进行研究。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 694, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/71/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 68.075, "size_bytes": 409392, "engine": "kokoro", "voice": "af_heart", "word_count": 233, "has_timeline": true, "alternates": [{"voice": "af_bella", "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/71/en_b1_af_bella.mp3", "duration": 70.675, "size_bytes": 424944, "engine": "kokoro", "word_count": 233, "has_timeline": true, "timeline_url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/timeline/71/en_b1_af_bella.json"}, {"voice": "am_michael", "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/71/en_b1_am_michael.mp3", "duration": 75.175, "size_bytes": 452016, "engine": "kokoro", "word_count": 233, "has_timeline": true, "timeline_url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/timeline/71/en_b1_am_michael.json"}, {"voice": "bf_emma", "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/71/en_b1_bf_emma.mp3", "duration": 64.8, "size_bytes": 389808, "engine": "kokoro", "word_count": 233, "has_timeline": true, "timeline_url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/timeline/71/en_b1_bf_emma.json"}, {"voice": "bm_george", "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/71/en_b1_bm_george.mp3", "duration": 69.8, "size_bytes": 419760, "engine": "kokoro", "word_count": 233, "has_timeline": true, "timeline_url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/timeline/71/en_b1_bm_george.json"}]}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@521b5e9/content/data/versions/703.json", "paragraphs": ["The mission is led by the Japanese Space Agency and launched from Japan on Monday. The spacecraft will reach Phobos in August 2027. If it succeeds, it will be the first time a sample from Mars or its moons comes back to Earth for study.", "Mars has two small moons, Phobos and Deimos. They are only about 20km and 10km across, and they look a bit like space potatoes. The spacecraft will study Deimos first, then move closer to Phobos.", "Landing there is not easy because Phobos has very little gravity of its own. However, the huge planet Mars is right next to it, and its strong pull makes the job harder. A small rover will look for a safe place to land.", "The spacecraft will then use a robotic arm to pick up moon dust. Only 10g will return, landing in South Australia in 2031. Scientists say even a tiny amount is enough, because every grain can tell them something new.", "The sample may answer a big question: where did Mars' moons come from? One idea is that a space rock hit Mars long ago, and the moons formed from the debris. Another is that they were asteroids caught by Mars' gravity."], "paragraphs_zh": ["这项任务由日本宇宙航空研究开发机构主导，探测器于周一从日本发射。它将在2027年8月抵达火卫一。如果成功，这将是火星或其卫星的样本首次被带回地球进行研究。", "火星有两颗小卫星——火卫一和火卫二，直径分别只有约20公里和10公里，形状不规则，看起来像太空土豆。探测器会先研究火卫二，然后靠近火卫一。", "在火卫一着陆并不容易，因为它自身的引力非常小。然而，巨大的火星就在旁边，其强大的引力让着陆更难。一台小型巡视器将先寻找安全的着陆点。", "随后，探测器会用机械臂采集月球尘土。最终只有10克样本返回，并于2031年在澳大利亚南部着陆。科学家说，哪怕量很少也足够了，因为每一粒尘土都能带来新信息。", "这些样本可能回答一个大问题：火星的卫星从哪来？一种看法是，很久以前一块太空岩石撞上火星，卫星由碎片形成。另一种看法是，它们是被火星引力捕获的小行星。"], "body": "The mission is led by the Japanese Space Agency and launched from Japan on Monday. The spacecraft will reach Phobos in August 2027. If it succeeds, it will be the first time a sample from Mars or its moons comes back to Earth for study.\n\nMars has two small moons, Phobos and Deimos. They are only about 20km and 10km across, and they look a bit like space potatoes. The spacecraft will study Deimos first, then move closer to Phobos.\n\nLanding there is not easy because Phobos has very little gravity of its own. However, the huge planet Mars is right next to it, and its strong pull makes the job harder. A small rover will look for a safe place to land.\n\nThe spacecraft will then use a robotic arm to pick up moon dust. Only 10g will return, landing in South Australia in 2031. Scientists say even a tiny amount is enough, because every grain can tell them something new.\n\nThe sample may answer a big question: where did Mars' moons come from? One idea is that a space rock hit Mars long ago, and the moons formed from the debris. Another is that they were asteroids caught by Mars' gravity.", "body_zh": "这项任务由日本宇宙航空研究开发机构主导，探测器于周一从日本发射。它将在2027年8月抵达火卫一。如果成功，这将是火星或其卫星的样本首次被带回地球进行研究。\n\n火星有两颗小卫星——火卫一和火卫二，直径分别只有约20公里和10公里，形状不规则，看起来像太空土豆。探测器会先研究火卫二，然后靠近火卫一。\n\n在火卫一着陆并不容易，因为它自身的引力非常小。然而，巨大的火星就在旁边，其强大的引力让着陆更难。一台小型巡视器将先寻找安全的着陆点。\n\n随后，探测器会用机械臂采集月球尘土。最终只有10克样本返回，并于2031年在澳大利亚南部着陆。科学家说，哪怕量很少也足够了，因为每一粒尘土都能带来新信息。\n\n这些样本可能回答一个大问题：火星的卫星从哪来？一种看法是，很久以前一块太空岩石撞上火星，卫星由碎片形成。另一种看法是，它们是被火星引力捕获的小行星。", "text": "The mission is led by the Japanese Space Agency and launched from Japan on Monday. The spacecraft will reach Phobos in August 2027. If it succeeds, it will be the first time a sample from Mars or its moons comes back to Earth for study. Mars has two small moons, Phobos and Deimos. They are only about 20km and 10km across, and they look a bit like space potatoes. The spacecraft will study Deimos first, then move closer to Phobos. Landing there is not easy because Phobos has very little gravity of its own. However, the huge planet Mars is right next to it, and its strong pull makes the job harder. A small rover will look for a safe place to land. The spacecraft will then use a robotic arm to pick up moon dust. Only 10g will return, landing in South Australia in 2031. Scientists say even a tiny amount is enough, because every grain can tell them something new. The sample may answer a big question: where did Mars' moons come from? One idea is that a space rock hit Mars long ago, and the moons formed from the debris. Another is that they were asteroids caught by Mars' gravity.", "vocab": [{"word": "launch", "pos": "verb", "zh": "发射", "note": "常指火箭、探测器发射", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "sample", "pos": "noun", "zh": "样本", "note": "科学语境中常见", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "moon", "pos": "noun", "zh": "卫星；月球", "note": "这里指行星的卫星", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "gravity", "pos": "noun", "zh": "引力；重力", "note": "描述天体时常用", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "land", "pos": "verb", "zh": "着陆", "note": "land on + 地点", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "rover", "pos": "noun", "zh": "巡视器；探测车", "note": "在星球表面移动的探测器", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "surface", "pos": "noun", "zh": "表面", "note": "on the surface of...", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "debris", "pos": "noun", "zh": "碎片；残骸", "note": "不可数名词", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "asteroid", "pos": "noun", "zh": "小行星", "note": "太阳系中的小天体", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "composition", "pos": "noun", "zh": "成分；构成", "note": "科学分析常用词", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Mission set to blast off for Mars to grab a piece of its moon", "summary_original": "A spacecraft is set to launch on an ambitious mission to bring 10g of Martian moon dust back to Earth.", "body_original": "Mission set to blast off for Mars to grab a piece of its moon\nBy\nRebecca Morelle\n,\nAlison Francis\nand\nKevin Church\n,\nBBC News Science\nPublished\n22 minutes ago\nA space mission is set to launch on Monday that will attempt to grab a piece of Mars' moon Phobos and bring it back to Earth.\nIf it is successful, this will become the first time a sample has been returned from Mars or its moons to be analysed in laboratories here on Earth.\nThe spacecraft will blast off from Japan and arrive at Phobos in August 2027.\nIt is hoped that the Mars Moons Exploration (MMX) mission, which is led by the Japanese Space Agency, will solve the mystery of how Mars's moons formed.\nProf Sara Russell, from the Natural History Museum, who is the MMX mission's deputy for sample analysis, said: \"There hasn't yet been a successful mission to go to Phobos and bring a bit back to Earth.\n\"I think this is an incredibly ambitious and fun mission,\" she said.\nThere are only three moons in the inner Solar System: our own Moon, and two around Mars, which are called Phobos and Deimos.\nBoth of these are tiny - Phobos is about 20km (12 miles) across and Deimos is about 10km (six miles) across. They are also irregularly shaped, looking a bit like space potatoes.\nThe MMX spacecraft will study Deimos as it arrives in the Mars neighbourhood, and then will focus on Phobos.\nIt plans to set down a small rover to explore the terrain, though this will be no easy feat.\nSara Russell says no one has ever landed a rover on such a small moon.\n\"The moon itself doesn't have very much of its own gravity,\" she explained.\n\"But it's next to an enormous planet that's got a much higher gravity, and that makes it much harder to get a rover onto the surface.\"\nThe rover will scout out a landing site so that the MMX spacecraft itself can later touch down on Phobos.\nOnce on the surface, it will deploy a robotic arm and two samplers to grab some moon dust.\nThis material will be packed into a small capsule that will return to Earth in 2031, landing in the Woomera desert of South Australia.\nThe amount of material that will come back is tiny - just 10g.\n\"It's a very small [amount] actually,\" said Dr Chrysa Avdellidou, from Leicester University, who is working with the Japanese mission.\n\"However we can do several studies even on individual grains… You don't need to have kilos back from a surface in order to do science. So it's worth every gram,\" she said.\nScientists believe the sample will finally shed light on the puzzle of where Mars's moons came from.\nThere are two competing ideas.\nEither a space rock smashed into Mars early in its history, and Phobos and Deimos were created from the debris.\nOr the moons are two wandering asteroids from the outer Solar System, which were grabbed by the Red Planet's gravity.\n\"When we get the sample back, we'll be able to look in detail at its chemistry and its mineral composition - and the two hypotheses will produce very distinct results,\" said Sara Russell.\n\"It should be pretty much immediately obvious.. whether it's the product of this dramatic impact or whether it's a captured asteroid. So we'll find that out really quickly.\"\nThe team is also hoping there might be an extra bonus coming back to their labs.\n\"One of the really exciting things is that Phobos is so close to the Mars surface that whenever there's an impact into the Mars surface, little grains might actually become implanted into Phobos itself,\" said Russell.\n\"And that means that the sample we get back may actually contain grains of Mars as well. So it can give us really direct information about the composition and history of Mars.\"\nBringing material from Mars back to Earth has long been an ambition of space scientists.\nThe Nasa Perseverance rover, which has been exploring Mars since 2021, has been carefully collecting samples on Mars and storing them in canisters.\nBut a second Nasa/ESA mission, called Mars Sample Return, that would have picked up and returned these canisters, has been effectively cancelled, after its funding was halted by the US Congress.\nChina is planning its own sampling mission to Mars, set to launch in 2028. It will attempt to return Martian material to Earth in 2031.\nWe do already have some fragments of the Red Planet here on Earth in the form of Martian meteorites.\nThese are pieces of Mars that were blasted off the surface by asteroid strikes, and ended up being hurled towards the Earth.\nMartian meteorites are extremely rare, but like all space rocks that fall to our planet, they become contaminated as soon as they get here, mixing with the air, dust, soil and water.\nScientists are so desperate to get their gloved hands on a direct sample from Mars and its moons because it would be pristine.\nThe MMX mission is scheduled to blast off from the Tanegashima Space Center at 0441 local time on 20 October (2041BST 19 October).\nRelated topics\nMars\nSpace exploration\nMars rovers\nScience & Environment", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T23:28:46.282887", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "The", "s": 0.275, "e": 0.35, "sm": 275, "em": 349, "cs": 0, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "mission", "s": 0.35, "e": 0.725, "sm": 350, "em": 725, "cs": 4, "ce":